12. Arguments for Protection
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: III | Topic: Economic and non-economic arguments for protection
Introduction
Free trade is efficient in theory, yet nearly all countries restrict imports in some way. Arguments for protection fall into economic and non-economic categories, with varying degrees of validity.
Economic arguments
1. Infant industry argument (Hamilton, List, Mill): new industries in developing countries need temporary protection to reach scale and learn. Valid in principle, but difficult to implement: protection often becomes permanent and favours inefficient firms. Subsidies are usually better.
2. Optimum tariff / terms-of-trade argument: a large country can improve its terms of trade by taxing imports, though it invites retaliation.
3. Balance of payments argument: tariffs to reduce imports and correct deficits. Better solved by exchange rate or macro policy.
4. Employment argument: protection preserves domestic jobs. This is a beggar-thy-neighbour policy, raises costs, and invites retaliation.
5. Anti-dumping / strategic trade policy: subsidies to firms in oligopolistic industries can capture rents (Brander-Spencer), though they risk subsidy wars.
6. Diversification: reduce vulnerability of economies reliant on a few exports.
7. Revenue argument: tariffs are an easy source of government revenue in low-income countries.
8. Domestic distortion argument: where externalities exist, intervention is justified, but first-best policy is a direct tax or subsidy, not a tariff.
9. Wage protection: to safeguard wages against low-wage imports (Stolper-Samuelson).
Non-economic arguments
• National defence and security: maintaining critical industries such as defence and energy.
• Self-sufficiency and food security.
• Preservation of culture and way of life.
• Health, environment and safety standards.
• Political bargaining power: tariffs as a negotiating tool.
Evaluation
Economists generally favour direct instruments over trade barriers, because protection imposes deadweight losses, raises consumer prices and is vulnerable to lobbying. The domestic-distortions theory shows that tariffs are, at best, a second-best remedy.
Conclusion
Some arguments, notably infant industry and security, have legitimate force, but protection should be targeted, temporary and transparent.